<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharwad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dharwad is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, forming a twin city with Hubballi. Its fort was held by the Adil Shahis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> before the British took the region in 1818. Seat of Karnatak University, founded in 1949, it is known as a centre of education and of Hindustani classical music, and it lent its name to the famous Dharwad pedha sweet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dharwad.jpg" alt="Dharwad" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kalpavruksha3363 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IIIT_Dharwad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
